        <abstract>
          <p>
            <bold>syn90024</bold>
          </p>
          <p><bold>Background: </bold>Early palliative care can improve symptom management, yet referral timing varies in metastatic breast cancer. We examined referral patterns following second-line progression. <bold>Methods: </bold>A retrospective review included 364 patients treated at two centers. Referral timing, symptom burden, emergency visits, and place of death were abstracted. Factors associated with referral within eight weeks of progression were assessed. <bold>Results: </bold>One hundred fifty-nine patients received a palliative care referral, with a median interval of 11 weeks after documented progression. Higher symptom burden and inpatient admission were associated with earlier referral, while age and tumor subtype were not. <bold>Conclusions: </bold>Referral frequently occurred after an acute-care encounter, indicating an opportunity for more systematic outpatient integration. Research Sponsor: None. </p>
        </abstract>
